  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What are heliostats?",
      "a": "Heliostats are flat mirrors on motorised stands that track the sun across the sky, so each one keeps reflecting sunlight onto the receiver at the top of the tower. A large plant can have over 10,000 of them.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are there so many mirrors?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How hot does the receiver get?",
      "a": "Concentrated sunlight can heat it to well over 500 degrees Celsius. It is a wall of metal tubes carrying molten salt, which soaks up the heat.",
      "asks": [
        "What is at the top of a solar tower?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why use molten salt?",
      "a": "A mix of sodium and potassium nitrate melts at around 220 degrees Celsius and can be heated to about 565 degrees without boiling. It stores lots of heat cheaply and loses little overnight in insulated tanks.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a solar tower store heat?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How is concentrated solar different from solar panels?",
      "a": "Solar panels turn light straight into electricity. Concentrated solar uses mirrors to make heat, then runs a steam turbine, which is more complicated but lets it store heat cheaply for use at night.",
      "asks": [
        "Is concentrated solar the same as solar panels?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where are solar power towers built?",
      "a": "They need strong, direct sunshine and clear skies, so they are built in sunny dry places such as southern Spain, Morocco, Chile, China, the Middle East and the south-western United States.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are solar towers in deserts?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Gemasolar in Spain, opened in 2011, was the first commercial solar tower to store heat in molten salt, giving up to about 15 hours of output without sunshine.",
    "The molten salt in solar towers is typically heated to about 565 degrees Celsius."
  ],
